WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.234 --> 00:00:12.942
On a warm night in August of 1921, Franklin Delano Roosevelt, also known as FDR, suddenly felt tired

2
00:00:13.023 --> 00:00:14.544
and decided to go to bed early.

3
00:00:15.385 --> 00:00:22.030
Vacationing on Campobello Island in Canada, the Roosevelt family had enjoyed a day of sailing and swimming, but

4
00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:25.173
by the next day, FDR had developed a high fever.

5
00:00:26.053 --> 00:00:32.579
Within weeks, FDR discovered he had polio, a highly infectious disease for which there was no vaccine or

6
00:00:32.659 --> 00:00:38.724
cure. From that moment, FDR knew that he would never walk, or even stand, without assistance.

7
00:00:39.504 --> 00:00:45.609
Fate had dealt a crushing blow to the 39-year-old politician, yet he would go on to inspire millions

8
00:00:45.649 --> 00:00:52.595
of people and become the only man to serve four consecutive terms as president of the United States.

9
00:00:52.655 --> 00:00:58.820
In 1882, FDR was born in Hyde Park, New York, into one of the wealthiest families in America.

10
00:00:59.584 --> 00:01:02.526
He was an independent boy who loved riding and sailing.

11
00:01:03.286 --> 00:01:08.929
Every winter, the Roosevelts would stay in their New York City townhouse, and in summer, they would vacation

12
00:01:08.989 --> 00:01:10.090
on Campobello Island.

13
00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:15.893
FDR was privately tutored and then attended boarding school at the age of 14.

14
00:01:16.793 --> 00:01:20.996
He entered Harvard University four years later and graduated in just three years.

15
00:01:22.056 --> 00:01:28.500
FDR greatly admired his fifth cousin, Theodore Roosevelt, who had been the 26th president of the United States.

16
00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:36.623
FDR entered state politics in 1910, but within two years, President Woodrow Wilson appointed him Assistant Secretary of

17
00:01:36.663 --> 00:01:42.705
the Navy, a position Theodore Roosevelt himself had held from 1897 to 1898.

18
00:01:43.786 --> 00:01:50.469
FDR proved himself an effective leader, and in 1916, he expanded American naval power by creating the United

19
00:01:50.529 --> 00:01:51.689
States Navy Reserves.

20
00:01:52.669 --> 00:02:00.128
Bright and talented, FDR was nominated to be the Democratic candidate for vice-president in 1920, and his popularity

21
00:02:00.168 --> 00:02:02.509
soared as Americans heard him on the campaign trail.

22
00:02:03.309 --> 00:02:09.711
But the Republican Party won the White House, and in 1921, after the grueling campaign across the country,

23
00:02:10.351 --> 00:02:12.392
FDR took a long-overdue vacation.

24
00:02:13.252 --> 00:02:17.713
At Campobello, doctors delivered the news that FDR would never again walk unaided.

25
00:02:18.453 --> 00:02:22.854
Polio was common around the world and killed or paralyzed thousands of people every year.

26
00:02:23.775 --> 00:02:28.716
FDR, determined not to surrender to paralysis, explored every type of therapy.

27
00:02:29.776 --> 00:02:35.500
In 1924, a friend told him about a resort in Warm Springs, Georgia, which had naturally heated mineral

28
00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:38.822
springs that his friend thought might help ease FDR's symptoms.

29
00:02:39.962 --> 00:02:45.686
FDR visited Warms Springs and was so impressed with the recuperative effects of the water that he turned

30
00:02:45.726 --> 00:02:49.648
it into a rehabilitation center for polio victims in 1927.

31
00:02:50.449 --> 00:02:55.512
He later founded the March of Dimes, an organization that raised money to eradicate the disease.

32
00:02:56.924 --> 00:03:02.446
In 1928, FDR wanted to run for governor of New York, but he worried about the public's perception

33
00:03:02.486 --> 00:03:03.767
of his physical disability.

34
00:03:04.527 --> 00:03:09.950
Fearing voters would consider him too weak to hold public office, FDR decided to conceal the true extent

35
00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:10.710
of his condition.

36
00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:15.332
He made campaign speeches from the back of a car with a hidden iron bar.

37
00:03:16.032 --> 00:03:20.154
When FDR wanted to address a crowd, he would grab the bar and pull himself up to a

38
00:03:20.194 --> 00:03:21.014
standing position.

39
00:03:21.754 --> 00:03:25.256
Then he would snap his metal leg braces into place to hold himself up.

40
00:03:26.044 --> 00:03:32.108
Newspaper photographers, by a gentleman's agreement, did not photograph FDR in a way that would reveal his disability.

41
00:03:33.029 --> 00:03:39.433
Unbelievably, of the tens of thousands of photographs taken of FDR throughout his life, only two exist that

42
00:03:39.473 --> 00:03:40.654
show him in his wheelchair.

43
00:03:41.835 --> 00:03:48.459
Soon after being elected governor, FDR faced a crisis when the American stock market crashed in 1929, and

44
00:03:48.499 --> 00:03:49.780
the Great Depression began.

45
00:03:50.501 --> 00:03:54.964
Bank failures, unemployment, hunger, and homelessness affected millions of Americans.

46
00:03:55.996 --> 00:04:01.698
So FDR decided to run for president of the United States; he believed the government should directly help

47
00:04:01.738 --> 00:04:09.680
suffering Americans by providing economic relief—something the administration of the current president, Herbert Hoover, refused to do.

48
00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:15.601
In 1932, FDR began the first of his four terms as president of the United States.

49
00:04:16.462 --> 00:04:21.523
He calmed and gave hope to the nation in his inaugural address when he spoke the memorable words

50
00:04:21.543 --> 00:04:24.424
"the only thing we have to fear is fear itself."

51
00:04:25.396 --> 00:04:30.858
In the first hundred days of his presidency, FDR worked with Congress to bring about an economic recovery,

52
00:04:31.218 --> 00:04:36.681
but there was strong resistance from opponents who thought the government should not create jobs for the unemployed.

53
00:04:37.521 --> 00:04:38.862
But FDR prevailed.

54
00:04:40.002 --> 00:04:45.884
He called his idea the "New Deal," and created programs that provided jobs and a safety net for

55
00:04:45.924 --> 00:04:50.986
people in the form of Social Security and a federal minimum wage.

56
00:04:51.087 --> 00:04:54.448
In 1936, FDR was elected to his second term.

57
00:04:55.220 --> 00:05:00.623
Americans had grown to trust FDR during the Great Depression, because he spoke often and frankly to them.

58
00:05:01.484 --> 00:05:07.407
He took advantage of the latest technology in mass communication, the radio, to broadcast what became known as

59
00:05:07.427 --> 00:05:09.968
"fireside chats" into millions of homes.

60
00:05:10.889 --> 00:05:16.732
His leadership was especially critical during this time because Nazi Germany was planning to invade its European neighbors,

61
00:05:17.072 --> 00:05:20.154
and Japan (an ally of Germany) would soon attack China.

62
00:05:21.074 --> 00:05:26.669
By the time FDR won his third term in 1940, Nazi Germany had invaded France and was intent

63
00:05:26.729 --> 00:05:27.810
on crushing Great Britain.

64
00:05:28.790 --> 00:05:33.832
FDR sent aid to England in the form of weapons and supplies, but there was political resistance to

65
00:05:33.872 --> 00:05:34.372
his efforts.

66
00:05:35.453 --> 00:05:38.234
His critics wanted the United States to stay out of the war.

67
00:05:38.954 --> 00:05:44.357
But when the Japanese suddenly attacked the American navy fleet at Pearl Harbor in Hawaii, war became inevitable.

68
00:05:45.117 --> 00:05:53.020
The next day, FDR began one of his most famous speeches to Congress, "Yesterday, December 7, 1941—a date

69
00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:58.822
which will live in infamy No matter how long it may take us to overcome this premeditated invasion,

70
00:05:59.202 --> 00:06:02.203
the American people will win through to absolute victory."

71
00:06:03.123 --> 00:06:08.345
He rallied the country to war, and the number of American men and women in military service swelled

72
00:06:08.365 --> 00:06:11.145
to over 12 million by mid-1943.

73
00:06:12.486 --> 00:06:19.508
In 1942, with Japan an enemy and hysteria from the attack sweeping the country, FDR signed an executive

74
00:06:19.648 --> 00:06:23.703
order that required the creation of internment camps for Japanese-American citizens.

75
00:06:24.964 --> 00:06:29.167
One hundred and twenty thousand innocent civilians were imprisoned for the duration of the war.

76
00:06:30.128 --> 00:06:36.593
Historians see this as an unfortunate stain on FDR's legacy, but many people were suspicious of other Americans

77
00:06:36.894 --> 00:06:40.056
who had ties to countries at war with the US.

78
00:06:40.156 --> 00:06:44.300
In 1988, the United States formally apologized to the families who were imprisoned.

79
00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:48.703
A visibly tired FDR was re-elected to his fourth term in 1944.

80
00:06:50.376 --> 00:06:55.899
Even as the war raged, FDR looked to the future and worked tirelessly to plan the reconstruction of

81
00:06:55.939 --> 00:06:57.440
the world after the war's end.

82
00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:04.083
He worked to establish an organization through which international conflicts could be resolved, which would become the United

83
00:07:04.143 --> 00:07:11.908
Nations. This organization called for a world based on Four Freedoms: Freedom of Speech, Freedom of Worship, Freedom

84
00:07:11.948 --> 00:07:13.789
from Want, and Freedom from Fear.

85
00:07:15.009 --> 00:07:20.132
But by March of 1945, the 63-year-old FDR was frail and exhausted.

86
00:07:20.896 --> 00:07:26.060
Years of fighting the effects of polio, coupled with the complexity of leading a nation in crisis, had

87
00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:27.481
taken a brutal toll on him.

88
00:07:28.321 --> 00:07:34.045
In need of rest, he traveled to Warm Springs and died there of a stroke on April 12,

89
00:07:34.105 --> 00:07:41.750
1945. A train carried FDR's casket to Washington DC, past millions of Americans who lined the railroad tracks

90
00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:42.951
in respectful silence.

91
00:07:43.892 --> 00:07:49.115
It was the end of the longest presidency in US history and the beginning of a new, postwar

92
00:07:49.176 --> 00:07:52.619
America, which had become the most powerful country in the world.
